      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I hear you, and while your Jenkins pipeline feels like it's been possessed by a mischievous goblin, the truth is likely a classic race condition or a hidden environmental variable. To isolate the ghost in the machine, start by enabling verbose logging in your test runner and capturing the full environment snapshot at the start of the flaky stage. You can also try running the tests in a clean, isolated container with no shared state, which will help you determine if a lingering artifact is the culprit. If the issue persists, consider adding a deterministic seed to your random number generators or using a CI-specific testing framework that handles flakiness better. Once you've narrowed down the variables, you can apply a patch to your build script to ensure consistency. Remember, sometimes the solution is as simple as a small configuration tweak, like adjusting the timeout or increasing the test isolation. If you're still stuck, don't hesitate to reach out to your team or consult the documentation. Good luck, and may your pipelines be as stable as a well-fed goblin!",
      "source": "target",
